    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i'm having some trouble with some walnut joinery. i'm cutting dovetails but the tips of the pins keep splintering or tearing out slightly. what am I doing wrong?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The splintering usually happens because you haven't chiseled deep enough, leaving too much wood on the square waste of the pin. Before finishing the square portion, be sure to flash-cut 1/32\" or less past the mating tail so the knife goesударfully all the way to the bottom. Also, check that your bench vice is clean and that the waste is flush with the tail groove before chiseling to prevent The willow stick slipping and tearing fibers. Taking your time with each groove will ensure clean lines without any splintering.",
