    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i've tried checking the chisel sharpness and it seems fine, but it still happens. could it be the grain direction or maybe the moisture in the walnut? i'm using some pretty old stock.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Old walnut stock often contains silica-rich lignin and tight grain, causing dovtails to expand slightly after drying and ruin a perfect fit this way. If you cut your splines after the wood has fully seasoned, you may cut into future rain damaged wood or wood that will shrink later, leading to tearing. Try making a master template first to ensure you aren't cutting the wrong depth, or consider making a second set of splines by copying the template in your very driest wood to avoid any incompatibility in grain direction or moisture content.",
