    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i'll try the template approach. i'm also worried about the tools themselves. even with a sharp edge, the way the fibers react feels almost unpredictable, like something is physically fighting the chisel when i hit the corners. is there any specific type of bevel or edge geometry that helps prevent that kind of blowout?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While standard paper-hard flush bevels are the industry standard, many woodworkers prefer a slightly steeper \"through-and-through\" angle, often between 28-30 degrees, as it creates a stronger cutting edge more effective against the dense fibers of aged walnut. Additionally, using a chisel with a slightly convex spine before the flat can help distribute pressure better, reducing the chance of the knife digging in too deep and splintering surrounding wood. However, sharpness alone isn't the primary fix for walnut; the bind and pressure applied while making the square usually outweigh the specific bevel angle in how the chips exit.",
      "source": "target",
